<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sochi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sochi is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Krasnodar</a> Krai, stretching along the shore below the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Before the Russian conquest the area was home to the Ubykhs and other Circassian peoples, and in 1861 it hosted the Circassian assembly that appealed for foreign help. After the expulsions of 1864 it was resettled and grew into a major Soviet and Russian resort, host of the 2014 Winter Olympics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sochi.jpg" alt="Sochi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trace · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sochi_harbour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muhammad Amin, a representative of Imam Shamil, organised courts and taxes among the Abadzekhs in the 1850s, and in 1861 delegates met at <b>Sochi</b> in a united assembly, often called the Circassian parliament, which appealed to Britain and the Ottoman Empire for support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It was part of a medley at the opening ceremony of the 2014 Winter Olympics in <b>Sochi</b>, and its title is inscribed on the pedestal of the bronze monument to Samantha Smith at Artek in Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/songs/may-there-always-be-sunshine">May There Always Be Sunshine</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1918 the Bolsheviks of Black Sea Governorate, a narrow coastal province including Novorossiysk, Tuapse and <b>Sochi</b>, proclaimed the Black Sea Soviet Republic as an autonomous part of the Russian SFSR during the early months of the Civil War.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-soviet-republic">Black Sea Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ubykhs lived on the Black Sea coast and mountain valleys around modern <b>Sochi</b>, between the Shapsugs and the Abkhaz.</blockquote><a href="/state/ubykhia">Ubykhia</a></li></ul></section>
